POSIX futex(2) shim
===================

User-facing surface for `SYS_futex` (slix syscall 182). Translates
musl's six-arg call (`uaddr, op, val, timeout_ts, uaddr2, val3`)
into operations on the kernel-side hash table in
`oskit/kernel/futex.lsysl`. The shim is responsible for:

  - validating user pointers and alignment,
  - reading `*uaddr` and comparing it to `val` (the racy-but-by-design
    futex precondition; the kernel running single-threaded between
    the compare and the park is exactly what makes the race close
    cleanly),
  - converting the user timespec into a 10-ms-tick deadline,
  - parking via `sleep_or_notify(1)` with the same EINTR /
    signal-pending check that `sys_ppoll` uses,
  - unwinding the kernel table on every exit path.

Sibling of `shim.lsysl` and `epoll.lsysl` — POSIX_FD constants,
errno values, and the LE-read helpers all resolve through the
shared `oskit.posix` module scope.

    module oskit.posix

    import oskit.kernel.{current_thread, kernel_futex_wait_enter,
        kernel_futex_wait_exit, kernel_futex_wake,
        kernel_futex_requeue, kernel_futex_self_parked,
        kernel_get_thread_ptbr,
        kernel_sigpending_get, kernel_sigmask_set, SIG_UNMASKABLE}
    import oskit.services.{sleep_or_notify, notify_read_self, monotonic_ms}
    import oskit.arch.{vm_v2p}


FUTEX op codes (match musl's `src/internal/futex.h`). The
private-mapping bit (`FUTEX_PRIVATE = 128`) and the
realtime-clock bit (`FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME = 256`) get masked off
before dispatching — slix doesn't distinguish private from
shared (there's only one process namespace right now) and treats
the timeout for `FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET` as absolute against
`monotonic_ms` regardless of which clock the caller picked.

    val FUTEX_WAIT          = 0
    val FUTEX_WAKE          = 1
    val FUTEX_REQUEUE       = 3
    val FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE   = 4
    val FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET   = 9
    val FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET   = 10
    val FUTEX_OP_MASK       = 127


Argument shape from musl. `uaddr` is a `*int32_t` (4-byte aligned);
`val` is the i32 the user expects to see at `*uaddr` (FUTEX_WAIT)
or the wake count (FUTEX_WAKE). For FUTEX_WAIT the fourth arg is a
relative `struct timespec*` (or NULL); for FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET it's
absolute. For FUTEX_(CMP_)REQUEUE the fourth arg is a *count*
(requeue_n), not a pointer.

The dispatcher in `shim.lsysl` forwards all six arg slots straight
through.

    sys_futex(uaddr_va: i64, op: int, expected: int,
              ts_or_count: i64, uaddr2_va: i64, bits3: int) -> i64
        if uaddr_va == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        if (uaddr_va & i64(3)) != i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)

        val base_op = op & FUTEX_OP_MASK

The shim runs in the caller's PTBR, so `vm_v2p` translates the
user address into a physical-page index using the caller's own
page table. If the address isn't mapped we surface `-EFAULT`.

        val ptbr = kernel_get_thread_ptbr(current_thread)
        val pa = vm_v2p(ptbr, u64(uaddr_va))
        if pa == u64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)

        if base_op == FUTEX_WAIT
            return futex_do_wait(uaddr_va, pa, expected, ts_or_count, u32(0xFFFFFFFF), false)
        if base_op == FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET
            if bits3 == 0
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            return futex_do_wait(uaddr_va, pa, expected, ts_or_count, u32(bits3), true)
        if base_op == FUTEX_WAKE
            return i64(kernel_futex_wake(pa, expected, u32(0xFFFFFFFF)))
        if base_op == FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET
            if bits3 == 0
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            return i64(kernel_futex_wake(pa, expected, u32(bits3)))
        if base_op == FUTEX_REQUEUE
            return futex_do_requeue(pa, uaddr_va, uaddr2_va, expected, int(ts_or_count), 0, false)
        if base_op == FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE
            return futex_do_requeue(pa, uaddr_va, uaddr2_va, expected, int(ts_or_count), bits3, true)
        i64(-ENOSYS)


`futex_do_wait` implements the FUTEX_WAIT and FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET
park-and-sleep half. The precondition compare is racy by design
(POSIX says the user does `cmpxchg` before calling, so a wake that
races with the call should be observable here as `*uaddr != val`).
We read the user word through its own page-table mapping, with no
TOCTOU guard between the read and the park — that's safe because
the kernel runs single-threaded between the two: any racing
`FUTEX_WAKE` from another thread can only run after this routine
yields (`sleep_or_notify`), and at that point the waiter is on the
bucket and reachable.

`absolute_deadline = true` means `ts_or_count` is a `*timespec`
holding an absolute deadline against `monotonic_ms` (which is what
musl's `__timedwait_cp` actually wants in the
`FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET|FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME` form — slix collapses the
distinction). `false` means relative.

    private futex_do_wait(uaddr_va: i64, pa: u64, expected: int,
                          ts_ptr: i64, bitset: u32,
                          absolute_deadline: bool) -> i64
        val up: *int = *int(uaddr_va)
        if *up != expected
            return i64(-EAGAIN)

        var deadline_ms = i64(-1)
        if ts_ptr != i64(0)
            val ts: *byte = *byte(ts_ptr)
            val tv_sec = shim_read_le_i64(ts, 0)
            val tv_nsec = shim_read_le_i64(ts, 8)
            if tv_sec < i64(0) || tv_nsec < i64(0) || tv_nsec >= i64(1000000000)
                return i64(-EINVAL)
            val timeout_ms = tv_sec * i64(1000) + tv_nsec / i64(1000000)
            if absolute_deadline
                deadline_ms = timeout_ms
            else
                deadline_ms = monotonic_ms() + timeout_ms

        kernel_futex_wait_enter(pa, bitset)
        var rc: i64 = i64(0)
        var done = false
        var iter = 0
        while !done && iter < 1000000
            iter += 1

A signal that the caller hasn't blocked is the equivalent of
EINTR — slix has no `SA_RESTART` yet, so every futex park is
interruptible. The `(pending & ~mask & ~UNMASKABLE)` shape mirrors
what `sys_ppoll` / `sys_pselect6` use; the signal walker on the
return path arms the handler, so the user sees `errno=EINTR` and
their normal `pthread_*` retry loops decide what to do.

            if (kernel_sigpending_get() & ~kernel_sigmask_set(-1, i64(0)) & ~SIG_UNMASKABLE) != i64(0)
                rc = i64(-EINTR)
                done = true
            else if *up != expected
                rc = i64(-EAGAIN)
                done = true
            else
                val _ = notify_read_self()

`kernel_futex_wake` cleared our `futex_addr_pa` and posted a
notification, so this branch only runs after a real wake — no
notification, no expiry. The `*uaddr != val` recheck above takes
care of the racy-overlap-with-wake case where the wake came in
between the precondition compare and the park.

                if kernel_futex_self_parked() == 0
                    rc = i64(0)
                    done = true
                else if deadline_ms >= i64(0) && monotonic_ms() >= deadline_ms
                    rc = i64(-ETIMEDOUT)
                    done = true
                else
                    sleep_or_notify(1)

        kernel_futex_wait_exit()
        rc


`futex_do_requeue` covers both `FUTEX_REQUEUE` and
`FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE`. The `cmp` variant adds a `*from == val3` guard
that musl's `pthread_cond_*` uses to detect spurious wakeups before
they cost a wake-and-requeue round-trip; mismatch returns
`-EAGAIN`. Both forms return the number of threads woken plus
requeued, matching Linux's contract.

    private futex_do_requeue(from_pa: u64, from_va: i64, to_va: i64,
                              wake_n: int, requeue_n: int,
                              cmp_val: int, do_cmp: bool) -> i64
        if to_va == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        if (to_va & i64(3)) != i64(0)
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if do_cmp
            val up: *int = *int(from_va)
            if *up != cmp_val
                return i64(-EAGAIN)
        val ptbr = kernel_get_thread_ptbr(current_thread)
        val to_pa = vm_v2p(ptbr, u64(to_va))
        if to_pa == u64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        i64(kernel_futex_requeue(from_pa, to_pa, wake_n, requeue_n))
